d.add_heading('Learning from Distracted Offline Data',0)
d.add_heading('Supervisor Work Summary',1)
p('Wasi alsafa\nDepartment of Computer Science and Engineering\nRajshahi University of Engineering & Technology\nRajshahi, Bangladesh\n31 October 2026')
chapter('Contents')
titles=['1 Foundations of Reinforcement Learning','2 Offline Learning and the Research Problem','3 Learning Progress and Literature Review','4 Initial Pipeline Validation','5 Comparative Study of Six Training Methods','6 Controlled Study of Gating and Alignment','7 Experimental Results and Their Meaning','8 Research Progress and Manuscript Preparation','9 Conclusions and Future Research']
for i,t in enumerate(titles,3):p(f'{t} ................................ {i}')
chapter(titles[0])
p('Reinforcement learning (RL) is a way of learning decisions through their consequences. An agent takes an action in an environment, receives a reward, and observes what happens next. It tries to learn a policy that produces a high total reward over time. For a running cheetah, the action controls its joints and the reward reflects progress on the running task.')
table(['Component','Simple explanation'],[['Agent','The decision-making model.'],['Environment','The world or simulator in which actions have effects.'],['State and observation','The state describes the underlying situation. An observation is what the agent receives, such as an image; it may hide part of the state.'],['Action','A decision, such as turning in Snake or applying joint controls.'],['Reward','A numerical signal indicating how useful an outcome is.'],['Policy','The rule or neural network that chooses actions from observations.'],['Value or Q function','An estimate of future reward from a state, or from a state and action.'],['Episode','One sequence from reset to termination or a time limit.']])
h('The learning objective')
p('A common objective is expected discounted return: G = r₀ + γr₁ + γ²r₂ + … . The discount factor γ controls how strongly future rewards count. A critic estimates future return; an actor chooses actions. In deep RL, neural networks represent the policy, value function or image encoder. An encoder turns pixels into a smaller set of learned features.')
h('Sequential Decisions and Learning from Experience')
p('Exploration means trying actions to discover their effects. Exploitation means using what has already been learned. The Markov assumption says that the current state contains the information needed to predict the next state given an action. A single image may not reveal velocity, so using several recent frames can help. The Bellman idea connects current value to immediate reward plus the value of what comes next.')
p('My Snake tutorial was a practical introduction to the agent–environment loop, actions, rewards and repeated training. It was a learning exercise, not evidence that the later visual offline method was already solved.')
chapter(titles[1])
p('Online RL collects new experience while learning. Offline RL learns from a fixed dataset collected earlier. A usual transition contains an observation, action, reward, next observation and terminal information. A replay buffer stores and samples these records. Offline learning is useful when new interaction is expensive or unavailable, but the learner cannot freely explore to correct mistakes.')
h('Why offline learning is difficult')
p('The data may contain only a limited range of actions or states. A learned value function can give unreliable estimates for actions that are poorly represented in the data. Behavioural cloning (BC) is a simpler baseline: it learns to copy recorded actions. It uses offline data but does not itself learn a reward-based value function. Offline RL methods often combine a reward-learning objective with a term that keeps actions close to the data.')
h('Research Focus on Visual Distractions')
p('We focus on visual representation learning. An image contains the cheetah and useful scene information, but also background videos, colours and camera changes. A model can fit the recorded data while relying on details that change at evaluation. We want it to retain information useful for control and rely less on distracting appearance. Training uses distracted observations only; avoiding a clean training dataset is a constraint, not proof of novelty.')
table(['Term','Meaning in this work'],[['Transition','One recorded environment step, not a complete game.'],['200 episodes / 100,000 transitions','In the second stage, 200 complete episodes each contribute 500 transitions.'],['Training seed','Controls random initialization and sampling; repeated seeds reveal run-to-run variation.'],['Evaluation seed','Controls a fresh simulator rollout and its random conditions.'],['Update','One optimization event using a sampled batch; many updates can reuse the same data.'],['Held-out source','Distraction sources reserved for evaluation rather than used as the familiar source set.']])
p('After training, the saved policy is placed in the simulator and chooses actions from new images. The simulator produces a trajectory and reward. This tests control behaviour; those evaluation transitions are not added to training. A rendered video can show behaviour, but scores across several episodes provide a more useful comparison.')
chapter(titles[2])
h('Practical Learning and Coursework')
p('I began with basic RL concepts, followed a tutorial to build a Snake-game agent, and started the UC Berkeley deep RL course on YouTube. The Snake exercise introduced the interaction between observations, actions and rewards. The coursework is ongoing. Alongside it, I have studied how the research code uses image encoders, actor–critic learning, replay buffers, behavioural cloning, loss functions, gradient updates, training seeds and checkpoints.')
h('Literature Reviewed and Relevance to the Project')
table(['Paper or method','What I took from it'],[['Chang et al., TMLR 2024','Domain-adversarial training uses a discriminator to discourage domain-specific visual features. It also uses DropBlock. Its limits include needing two datasets and understanding how their differences affect results. It already discusses two distracted datasets.'],['V-D4RL, Lu et al., TMLR 2023','A benchmark for offline control from images; supplies useful baselines and tests of visual distraction.'],['DrQ-v2, Yarats et al., ICLR 2022','Efficient visual actor–critic learning with augmentation. Its offline adaptation must be distinguished from the original online method.'],['SeMOPO, Wan et al., ICML 2024','Separates agent-related and external factors in a world model so distractors do not dominate uncertainty. We did not train a world model.'],['Synthetic Data, Güzel et al., TMLR 2025','Uses visual diversity and generated latent data to improve generalization. Our RGB augmentation pilot is much simpler.'],['ACRO, Islam et al., ICML 2023','Predicting actions from current and future observations can encourage control-related features.'],['InfoGating, Tomar et al., NeurIPS 2023','A learned gate restricts information while retaining a task prediction objective. We adapted author components.'],['MMCS, Zhang et al., KBS 2025','A related representation approach with latent masks and sufficiency objectives. We inspected author code and accessible publisher information; the full paywalled paper was not obtained.']])
p('Ganin et al. (JMLR 2016), the Distracting Control Suite (Stone et al., 2021), and TD3+BC (Fujimoto and Gu, 2021) provide background for gradient reversal, distraction generation and behavioural regularization. Reviewing a method is different from reproducing its published results. Full bibliographic details and links are in the manuscript and repository reference notes.')
chapter(titles[3])
h('Question and purpose')
p('The first experiment asked: can this computer load the dataset, update a model, save it and evaluate it correctly? It was deliberately a smoke test—a small end-to-end check—rather than an attempt to train a strong cheetah policy. It completed on 10 October 2026.')
table(['Item','Recorded setup'],[['Training data','10,000 transitions from a single distracted subset; no clean training data.'],['Method','DrQv2+BC baseline with one replay buffer.'],['Batch size','16.'],['Training','400 loop steps, producing 200 optimizer-update events.'],['Acceptance checks','All 20 recorded checks passed.'],['Outputs','Logs, checkpoints, simulator render/video checks and a supervisor report.']])
h('What was checked')
p('The checks covered GPU computation, data shapes and values, complete episodes, frame stacking, reward calculations, bounded actions, finite losses and changing encoder/actor/critic parameters. Saving and reloading a checkpoint was also checked. Normal and distracted simulator rendering were tested, and evaluation was kept out of the training buffer.')
h('What it showed')
p('The pipeline could run from input data to simulator evaluation. This gave us a practical base for longer experiments. Passing these checks did not show visual robustness, convergence or superiority over another algorithm. With only 200 update events, the test was too small to support those conclusions.')
h('Code issues and lessons')
p('The smoke-test documentation records a gradient-reversal regression check and an upstream gradient-reversal issue. The single-buffer baseline did not use that adversarial objective. The later hybrid implementation tested its own gradient paths, so it should be described as an adapted implementation rather than an unchanged reproduction of the original paper.')
p('The smoke folder is large because it contains an interpreter, dependencies, selected data, caches and outputs as well as code. That size should not be confused with the amount of source code. The smoke project was preserved separately at E:\\offline_rl_smoke_test.')
chapter(titles[4])
p('The second stage compared six approaches on the same easy-distraction cheetah subset: 100,000 transitions from 200 complete episodes, selected as 25 episodes from each of eight shards. Each method used three training seeds and 1,200 measured update seconds per main run. Equal time does not mean equal update counts or equal convergence.')
table(['Method','What it tests','Held-out return'],[['BC','Can copying actions work?','7.68 ± 5.99'],['DrQv2+BC','Reward-learning baseline with BC','1.81 ± 0.70'],['Stronger RGB augmentation','Does extra colour variation help?','19.63 ± 14.91'],['Two-view policy consistency','Does agreement across views help?','9.98 ± 11.78'],['Fixed top-band masking','Does covering one region help?','6.26 ± 5.77'],['Adapted InfoGating','Does a learned gate help?','59.74 ± 57.04']])
p('Each ordinary score averages three evaluation episodes per trained seed, then averages the three seed means. Adapted InfoGating had the highest held-out mean in this pilot, but also very large variation. Its architecture and objective differed from the DrQv2+BC variants. This was a reason to investigate further, not proof that gating alone caused the difference.')
h('Other tests in this stage')
p('A data-size pilot compared BC and DrQv2+BC with 10,000 versus 100,000 transitions using 300 update seconds per run. Held-out BC means were 7.55 and 3.40; DrQv2+BC means were 7.01 and 10.87. The short runs and large variation do not establish a reliable benefit from more data. The full second stage contained 30 runs, about 7.0001 update hours and 234 evaluation episodes.')
p('We also covered central image bands during evaluation to test sensitivity to missing information. Results were mixed and used only one evaluation seed per damage condition. Fixed masking was not object segmentation, and a band could remove useful parts of the cheetah or scene. This helped motivate checking failure cases instead of assuming that background removal must help.')
h('Why we moved to a controlled design')
p('The six-method pilot mixed different objectives, networks and update counts. It could screen ideas but could not isolate the effect of adding gating to alignment. The next stage therefore kept the main learning rule fixed and changed only whether gating and alignment were enabled. Earlier pilot scores must not be directly pooled with the later study.')
chapter(titles[5])
p('The main question became: does action-guided information gating prevent domain-adversarial alignment from losing useful control information when training only on distracted data? We tested the interaction rather than assuming that combining two existing methods would improve performance.')
table(['Condition','Gating','Alignment','Shared learning rule'],[['Inv','No','No','Inverse action prediction + BC'],['IG','Yes','No','Inverse action prediction + BC'],['DA+Inv','No','Yes','Inverse action prediction + BC'],['DA+IG','Yes','Yes','Inverse action prediction + BC']])
p('Inverse prediction asks the representation to help identify the recorded action from current and later images. A gate learns which image regions to retain during training. A domain classifier predicts which visual domain the features came from; reversed gradients encourage the encoder to make that prediction harder. The BC policy learns recorded actions from the features. It receives raw images at evaluation, following the adopted author-code convention.')
h('Data and budget')
p('We used easy and medium distracted cheetah-run medium-expert data. In each source shard, 25 complete episodes were divided into 20 training, 3 probe-fit and 2 probe-test episodes. Across both domains this gave 160,000 training, 24,000 probe-fit and 16,000 probe-test transitions. No clean training observations were used. Each batch contained eight samples from each domain.')
p('All four conditions used three seeds and reached 40,000 updates: 12 completed runs. The batch size was 16 and the learning rate was 0.0001. Measured update time was about 6.376 hours on a GTX 1650 with 4 GiB memory; preparation and evaluation time are excluded. Checkpoints were saved every 2,500 updates with model weights, optimizers and random states.')
h('What this experiment actually is')
p('This is a controlled offline imitation and representation study. It has no reward-learning critic. It does not reproduce the full original domain-adversarial RL method and cannot establish superiority over that paper’s published scores. The adapted author inverse model generates 256 random negative actions independently of batch size; an earlier suggestion that batch 16 meant only 16 contrastive negatives was corrected.')
chapter(titles[6])
p('Performance is measured by episode return, the sum of rewards collected during one simulator episode. Higher return indicates better control on this task. Each mean ± SD below is calculated across three training-seed means. SD shows variation between trained models; it is not a confidence interval.')
table(['Condition','Familiar return','Held-out return','Action probe MSE'],[['Inv','115.06 ± 21.78','142.36 ± 45.05','0.1518'],['IG','121.41 ± 47.27','129.18 ± 8.81','0.1570'],['DA+Inv','92.24 ± 57.96','157.73 ± 38.63','0.1499'],['DA+IG','106.27 ± 14.97','109.61 ± 9.51','0.1539']])
p('The final static-distraction test used five fresh evaluation seeds per setting and trained model. There were 120 final episodes and 120 intermediate episodes. Final checkpoints were chosen by the fixed update budget, not by selecting the best test score. The ± values describe three training-seed means, not fifteen independent trained models.')
h('Effect of Adding Gating to Alignment')
p('Adding gating to alignment lowered the held-out score in all three matched training seeds. The average paired difference was −48.11 reward units, with sample SD 29.13. The training pipeline worked, but this primary test did not support the expected improvement from the combined method. Three seeds and one task are insufficient for a broad conclusion that gating is always harmful.')
h('Independent Tests of Learned Features')
p('We froze each trained encoder and fitted separate simple prediction models on probe-fit episodes, then evaluated on probe-test episodes. Lower action-prediction MSE is better. DA+IG had higher error than DA+Inv. All four conditions had domain-prediction accuracy 1.0, compared with balanced chance accuracy 0.5. Domain information therefore remained easy to recover; we cannot claim domain-invariant features or guaranteed preservation of useful control information. Training loss alone would not have shown this clearly.')
table(['Exploratory fresh-seed test','Static return','Moving return'],[['Inv','77.92 ± 38.01','70.53 ± 37.67'],['IG','95.03 ± 4.82','61.02 ± 5.85'],['DA+Inv','46.76 ± 20.21','73.18 ± 19.81'],['DA+IG','88.61 ± 38.32','79.05 ± 4.05']])
p('This separate 120-episode test changed background, camera and colour together. The mean ranking reversed: DA+IG was above DA+Inv here. Even the static scores changed with the fresh evaluation seeds. This limits the primary finding to its evaluation set and calls for broader testing. It does not identify which distraction caused the change or establish a reliable advantage.')
chapter(titles[7])
h('Overall Research Progress')
p('The work has progressed through three stages: validating an offline training pipeline, comparing six methods, and testing gating and alignment in a controlled design. The final stage is the main study used in the manuscript. Its primary result did not support the expected improvement from adding gating, while the separate moving-distraction test showed that performance depends on the evaluation conditions.')
h('Data Verification and Experiment Recovery')
p('Data audits checked 48 split files, shapes, finite values, action bounds, episode boundaries and hashes. Episode selections were disjoint within each source. An exact action-sequence audit found no identical 500-step sequences across splits; related or approximately repeated physical trajectories could still remain. Independent probes used separate episodes, but their sampled pairs can repeat, so they are not thousands of independent trajectories.')
p('Completed checkpoints and evaluation records were checked. A computer restart happened after training had finished and interrupted probe computation. Missing probes were resumed, without restarting completed training. Export verification checks saved file hashes and return arithmetic. These checks support traceability, but cannot prove that every scientific assumption or simulator setting is correct.')
h('Code and records')
p('The project is at E:\\offline_rl. Training and analysis scripts are under scripts/hybrid; small result records are under results/hybrid_20261030. The earlier comparison remains under results/baseline_comparison. The GitHub repository is https://github.com/wasi-coder/offline-visual-rl-distractions. Large datasets, environments and checkpoints are excluded from ordinary Git history. Applicable upstream licences and attribution were preserved; author InfoGating source with unresolved licence terms is not redistributed.')
h('Manuscript Development and Current Status')
p('The manuscript is titled “Information Gating Under Domain Adversarial Alignment in Distracted Offline Visual Control.” We prepared an IEEE double-column version with tables, original plots, references and a clear limitations section. The paper reports the negative primary result and the different exploratory ranking. It includes an AI-assistance disclosure. Author details identify Wasi alsafa and Sadad Kadery, CSE, Rajshahi University of Engineering & Technology.')
p('After receiving the ECCE 2027 initial-submission rules, we made a separate anonymous PDF by removing names, affiliations, contact details, identifying repository references and author metadata. It is five pages, within the six-page limit. The named copy is retained separately. Track 6, Artificial Intelligence and Data Science, best matches the main topic among the supplied tracks.')
p('The manuscript and anonymous review copy have been prepared. Submission, acceptance and publication are not confirmed in the project records. The remaining submission tasks include entering author details in CMT, obtaining author approval and completing the required similarity check. The paper is co-authored with Sadad Kadery. AI assistance supported implementation, experiment orchestration, analysis and drafting; the reported performance values come from saved experimental records.')
chapter(titles[8])
table(['Priority','Next step','Why it matters'],[['First','Review the implementation and explain every loss and gradient path.','I must be able to defend the work independently and distinguish BC from reward-based RL.'],['First','Confirm submission status and complete author/venue checks.','A prepared PDF is not an accepted or published paper.'],['Next','Add independent training and evaluation seeds under a fixed plan.','Tests whether the observed direction is repeatable; more seeds do not guarantee significance.'],['Next','Test alignment strengths and classifier capacity.','Domain identity remained fully decodable; the intended invariance was not demonstrated.'],['Next','Inspect learned masks and compare raw versus gated inference.','Checks whether the training gate keeps useful regions and whether inference conventions matter.'],['Later','Compare a faithful original-method baseline under matched conditions.','Separates implementation/protocol changes from a possible method contribution.'],['Later','Extend to more tasks, data sizes and distraction types.','One cheetah task cannot establish general robustness.']])
h('Ideas that remain proposals')
p('Possible extensions include combining learned gating with a fixed spatial mask, domain-adversarial training with stronger action-preservation objectives, better background removal, and consistency between two views. Fixed regions can remove useful information and semantic masks can fail. These ideas need component controls and failure tests; they are not completed or validated solutions.')
p('A useful demonstration would render the trained cheetah policy running in the simulator and compare behaviours under several distractions. This would help explain the work to a supervisor, but a good-looking video should accompany systematic scores rather than replace them. The physical-robot idea was dropped from the current scope.')
h('Conclusion')
p('The project has established a working offline visual-control pipeline and a documented comparison of representation-learning methods. The controlled experiment found no primary-test benefit from adding learned gating to adversarial alignment, and domain information remained recoverable from every representation. The different ranking in the exploratory test shows that broader evaluation is necessary. These results provide a basis for further study of action preservation, alignment strength and robustness across visual conditions.')
h('Evidence used for this report')
p('The experimental account is based on the smoke-test records, archived baseline evaluations, hybrid training summaries, independent probe results and the secondary distraction test. These records are retained with the project code and manuscript files for review and reproduction.')
